Line team,

From tomorrow's first shift, Greenleaf salad kits change as follows.

Each kit carries a single cold pack of 800 g, laid flat on top of the salad tubs, not under them. Greenleaf's leaves bruise and go limp if they touch anything very cold, so the pack sits on the cardboard tray above them, never in contact with a tub.

The pack is a phase-change type that melts at +5 C. Each pcm unit is conditioned in the line cold room for at least 18 hours before it goes in a kit; the rack for conditioned packs is the one nearest the door, marked with the green tag.

Check every pack for leaks and swelling as you lay it in. A leaking or swollen one goes in the red crate under the bench, and the line lead counts that crate at the end of the shift.

Do not use any brick, gel pack or ice pack from another programme's racks, even if ours run short. If they run short, stop the line and tell the line lead.

The kitting sheet on each station has been updated with the new photo. If anything is unclear, ask before the shift starts, not halfway through.

Rohit
